The '''Balboa Canal''' is an artificial [[lock (water navigation)|lock]]-based waterway that traverses the [[Isthmus of Balboa]], connecting the [[Aethiopian Ocean]] to the [[Kendriyan Ocean]]. At approximately 82 kilometres in length, it is one of the most important commercial waterways on [[Adonia]] and the single most significant economic asset of the [[Republic of Balboa]]. The canal eliminates the need for shipping to navigate around the continents, saving approximately 12,000 kilometres on a typical voyage between the Aethiopian and Kendriyan coasts of Lurandia.

The canal was constructed by the [[Balboa-Volisanian Company]] and completed in 1885. It was transferred to [[Kingdom of Zong|Zong]] after the [[Second War of the Isthmus]] (1893–1899), operated by the Zong administration during the [[Zong Balboa]] period (1899–1950), and placed under international administration during the AC mandate period (1950–1973). The [[Treaty of Areza]] (1973) returned the canal to Balboan sovereignty, and it is now operated by the [[Balboa Canal Authority]], a Balboan state agency, under the treaty's non-discrimination and access provisions.

[[File:Balboa Canal lock gates.jpg|thumb|right|300px|The lock gates at the Aethiopian entrance to the canal.]]

== Route and layout ==

The canal traverses the [[Isthmus of Balboa]] from east to west, following a route that exploits the natural topography of the Yalabirga highland depression. The eastern (Aethiopian) terminus is at [[Sant Llàtzer]], where vessels enter the canal from the harbour on the Aethiopian coast. The canal then proceeds westward through the Estret lowlands, ascending through the first set of locks to the level of [[Lake Alvarado]], an artificial lake created by the damming of the central highland basin. The lake, which occupies the central reach of the canal at an elevation of approximately 26 metres above sea level, extends for roughly 35 kilometres through the highland valley. The [[International City of Areza]] is situated on the north shore of Lake Alvarado.

West of the lake, the canal descends through two further sets of locks to the Kendriyan terminus at [[Portnou]]. The western approach channel cuts through the lower Yalabirga ridge via a short excavated section before reaching the Kendriyan coastal plain. Portnou was selected as the canal's western terminus in preference to [[Sant Cristòfor]] — the older and larger city on the Kendriyan coast — because its harbour offered superior depth, shelter, and capacity for the expansion of port facilities.

The canal's three lock sets are:

